# Meshploy Caddyfile - generated, do not edit.
#
# Rendered by `meshploy domain apply` from the edge snapshot at
# /var/lib/meshploy/host/state/edge.json.
# Every edit here is lost the next time a base domain is added, removed,
# verified, or has its DNS mode changed. Put your own configuration in
# conf.d/*.caddy, which is imported below and never written.
#
# Caddy runs with network_mode: host, so every reverse_proxy target is on the
# loopback address. Written 127.0.0.1, never "localhost": some images map
# localhost to ::1 alone, and the services behind are published on IPv4.
#
# `tls force_automate` on a platform subdomain of an on-demand domain is not
# decoration. That domain's wildcard is on-demand, so it is never obtained up
# front, and Caddy prefers a covering wildcard to an individual certificate -
# leaving the named site in a gap where nothing issues its certificate and the
# console is unreachable over HTTPS on a fresh install. A delegated domain does
# not need it: its wildcard is a real certificate that genuinely covers them.
#
# An on-demand domain with a DNS provider is arranged as a delegated one is:
# its wildcards are obtained up front by DNS-01, answered through the
# provider's API. The token is not in this file. {file.*} names where Caddy
# reads it, under secrets/, which only root can read.

{
    on_demand_tls {
        # One ask endpoint for the whole server, because on_demand_tls is a
        # global option and cannot vary per site. It approves an active workload
        # route under any verified base domain, and a verified custom domain -
        # the two things that may be issued without being named here.
        # The API runs on another machine and is asked over the mesh. Only a
        # new hostname needs it: certificates already issued keep serving
        # while it is away.
        ask http://100.64.0.1:4000/api/v1/internal/ondemand-tls-check
    }
}

# The platform's API, console and Headscale run on another machine, which
# serves their names itself. This edge serves the apps alone.

# Anything you add yourself. Imported before the site blocks, so a syntax error
# in it fails the config outright rather than at the first request.
import conf.d/*.caddy

# ═══ example.com - NS delegation, primary ═══

# ── Public workload routes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The proxy reads the Host header, looks the route up, and forwards to the
# target node over the mesh.
*.example.com {
    # One wildcard certificate by DNS-01, written into this domain's own
    # challenge zone.
    tls {
        dns meshploy {
            zone_file_path /etc/coredns/zones/_acme-challenge.example.com
        }
    }
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}

# ── Internal mesh traffic ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bound to the mesh address, so it is reachable only from inside the mesh.
*.internal.example.com {
    bind 100.64.0.2
    # A public wildcard needs DNS-01, which needs a zone the world can query.
    # resolvers points CertMagic at the authoritative CoreDNS directly, so no
    # CNAME is chased and no recursive cache stands in the way.
    tls {
        dns meshploy {
            zone_file_path /etc/coredns/zones/_acme-challenge.internal.example.com
        }
        resolvers 203.0.113.10
    }
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}

# ═══ second.test - on-demand, records through cloudflare ═══

# ── Public workload routes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The proxy reads the Host header, looks the route up, and forwards to the
# target node over the mesh.
*.second.test {
    # One wildcard certificate by DNS-01, written through the provider.
    tls {
        dns cloudflare {file./etc/caddy/secrets/0b6f3c2e-6a1d-4f7e-9a51-2c8d4e7f1a90}
    }
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}

# ── Internal mesh traffic ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bound to the mesh address, so it is reachable only from inside the mesh.
*.internal.second.test {
    bind 100.64.0.2
    # The challenge record goes in the provider's zone, which the world can
    # query, so an internal name gets a public certificate like any other.
    tls {
        dns cloudflare {file./etc/caddy/secrets/0b6f3c2e-6a1d-4f7e-9a51-2c8d4e7f1a90}
    }
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}

# ── Custom domains ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any hostname none of the blocks above matched, such as a customer's own domain
# on a route. The ask endpoint gates issuance, so only a hostname whose
# ownership was proved gets a certificate.
:443 {
    tls {
        on_demand
    }
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}
